@meta
id: 17-09
title: Walking the changelist both ways
chapter: 17
type: lesson
skills: changelist
par: 62
minutes: 7
vscode: no equivalent beyond one step back
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
The gateway has moved to `/v2`. All three paths in this service say
`/v1`; change them, top to bottom. Search once and let `n` and the dot
command do the other two.

Then a second job that sends you back through your own edits. `capture`
has no status check. `charge` has the right one: the `if` block after
its request, with the blank line that follows it. Copy those four lines
from `charge`, put them into `capture` after its blank line, and change
the error code in the copy to `CAPTURE_FAILED`.

You are standing on the third `v2` when the first job ends. The block
you need is two lines under the first `v2`, and the place it goes is
just under the third. Use the changelist to get to both, in both
directions, and use `:changes` to see the numbers before you press
anything.

@keys
| `g;` | older change; takes a count |
| `g,` | newer change; takes a count |
| `:changes` | the changelist, newest at the bottom, `>` where you are |
| `n` `.` | next match, repeat the change |
| `y3j` | yank this line and the three below |

@hints
After three edits the changelist has three entries, and you are on the
newest. `:changes` shows the count you need to reach the oldest.
--
`3g;` lands on the first `v2`. From there the block is `2j` down, and
`y3j` takes it with its blank line. `2g,` then goes forward two changes
to the third `v2`.
--
`/v1` Enter, `ciwv2` Esc, `n.` `n.`, `3g;` `2jy3j` `2g,` `jp`, then
`j` `fC` `ciwCAPTURE_FAILED` Esc.

@solution
    /v1<Enter> ciwv2<Esc> n. n.
    3g; 2jy3j 2g, jp jfCciwCAPTURE_FAILED<Esc> :wq<Enter>

`g;` and `g,` are `Ctrl-O` and `Ctrl-I` for the changelist. Older and
newer, and both take a count. `:changes` is `:jumps` for the same list
and reads the same way: newest at the bottom, `>` where you are, the
number in the left column being the count to give.

Why this beat the jumplist. The three `n` presses were jumps, so the
jumplist also held the three positions, mixed in with the initial
search. The changelist held *only* the three edits, in order, with
nothing else in it, so `3g;` meant "the first thing I changed" with no
arithmetic.

The yank in the middle mattered. Yanking does not touch the changelist,
so after `y3j` the list was intact and `2g,` still made sense. Had you
made any edit at the first `v2`, that edit would have become the newest
entry and `g,` would have had nowhere to go. When you need to visit
several of your own edits, do the reading and copying first and the
new edits last.

@start payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async charge(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/v1/charges', args);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CHARGE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async refund(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/v1/refunds', args);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async capture(args: { orderId: string }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/v1/captures', args);

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async charge(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/v2/charges', args);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CHARGE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async refund(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/v2/refunds', args);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async capture(args: { orderId: string }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/v2/captures', args);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CAPTURE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}
